The system was failing to automatically fetch and update attendance records (specifically check_out times and work hours) from the source table (attendance_db.attendance_daily_summary) to the target table (atten.attendance). Even when the script was run, records like Dali Pal (FNM1924) remained stuck with old data.
Technical Root Causes
Multiple Unique Index Conflict: The target table had 6 redundant unique indexes (uk_person_date, unique_attendance, etc.) which caused MySQL's ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE logic to behave unpredictably.
ID Matching Issues: Subtle differences in data collation or trailing spaces in person_id prevented the script from recognizing existing rows.
Manual Execution Requirement: The system relied on a user manually opening a browser URL to trigger the sync, leading to data delays.
? The Solution
1. Database Optimization
We identified and removed redundant indexes to ensure a clean path for the sync logic.
Kept: PRIMARY and uk_person_date.
Removed: All other redundant idx_ and unique_ combinations.
2. Improved Sync Logic (attendance_sync.php)
The script was redesigned to be "forceful" and optimized:
TRIM Logic: Uses TRIM(person_id) to ensure matching even if hidden spaces exist.
Date Windowing: Instead of syncing the whole month, the script now syncs only the last 2 days. This makes it run in milliseconds.
Dual-Action Sync:
Step 1: Updates existing records (for check-outs).
Step 2: Inserts new records (for new arrivals).
Step 3: Refreshes Status (Present/Late/Absent) and calculates Work Hours.
?? Automation (The Cron Job)
The system is now fully automated using a server-side Cron Job. It no longer requires manual browser refreshing.
Cron Configuration
The task is scheduled via PuTTY using the crontab -e command.
Schedule: Every 2 minutes (*/2 * * * *)
Execution Command:
code
Bash
/usr/bin/php /srv/www/htdocs/attendance/includes/modules/attendance/attendance_sync.php >> /var/log/attendance_sync.log 2>&1
?? Monitoring & Logs
You can monitor the live health of the sync system via PuTTY.
To check if the sync is running:
Run the following command to see the real-time log:
code
Bash
tail -f /var/log/attendance_sync.log
What a successful log looks like:
code
Text
--- ATTENDANCE SYNC START ---
Syncing data for dates >= 2026-08-20
Step 1: Sync finished. Rows affected: 39
Step 2: Work hours updated: 19
Step 3: Status updated.
--- SYNC COMPLETED SUCCESSFULLY ---
?? Maintenance Tips
Port Changes: The current setup uses a direct PHP call on the server. If you move the file to a different folder, remember to update the path in crontab -e.
Warnings: You may see session_start() warnings in the log file. These are harmless and occur because the script is running from the Terminal (CLI) rather than a browser.
Data Integrity: If data stops appearing, check the attendance_db (source) first to ensure it is receiving data from the biometric devices.
Status: ACTIVE & AUTOMATED
Frequency: Every 2 Minutes
Sync Window: Last 48 Hours